A||||8||Gokart||Winterizing!||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||08-29-2000||09:52 PM||||I'm on vacation next week and will be getting my GT winter ready. I've been under the car recently and noticed a few spots I missed with rubberized undercoating last year and a few that have light rust. I'll go to town with wire brushes/wheels and dremmels first on the rust then wanted some advice. Instead of the rubber stuff in a spray has anyone ever thought of using Rust-oleum paint products instead? Maybe brush a coat of primer then brush a coat of paint? Don't have the funds for any fancy schmancy stuff sold by JC, etc. So it's either paint or rubberized! Your advice? Man, I wish I had a garage  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gokart_sig_1.gif">  <P>Marc                     87GT<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 08-29-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||08-29-2000||11:16 PM||||you need to kill the rust. That's best done with one of the many stop rust products. leave the rust in place and let the chemical do it's job. You can them paint or what ever over that. It's better than trying to sand it off. You'll never get it all off that way.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||08-29-2000||11:33 PM||||Winterizing?...it hit 109 degrees here today..set an all time high, even for Memphis...jeez, Im SO ready for winter!||||reg||11
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-30-2000||04:35 AM||||I'll never be ready for winter!  75 during the day and 55 at night year round would be nice.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-30-2000||08:50 AM||||I agree with Theogre on treating the rust.  WalMart sells a can of spray called Rust Converter that works pretty good.  Remove loose scale and spray the rest.  It converts the iron oxide to iron phosphate.  The rusty area turns black and is paintable.<P>The spray is thin and clear.  It is great for treating rust in cracks and crevices that you'd have a hard time getting to otherwise.  I believe a can costs about $4 or $5 and is found in the automotive paint section by the bondo.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000005||TheFiero64||08-30-2000||01:53 PM||||I want to try some of that por-15 stuff. If I do I will let you know how it works.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||webbee||08-30-2000||03:25 PM||||I usually sand, use the rust converter, paint liquid fiberglass, then use the undercoat on the undercarrage. Stop rust, seal, and protect. Tape off anything that you might want to remove later.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||08-30-2000||05:01 PM||||I would sand as much of the loose rust off as I could before using the chemical.  Then use the rust killer.  After that, I see no problem using a rustoleum primer on it.  There is also a primer with one of those rust killing chemicals in it that I have used before.  It gets runny real quick but seemed to help.  I am personally kind of against that rubberized undercoating because it can plug important drain holes and such.  Of course if your putting it on yourself it should be ok 'cause you can be careful.  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/Motorcity/Boulevard/9278/fierosig1.jpg">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||08-30-2000||09:10 PM||||Thanks guys. I have had good success with Extend by Permatex so I'll get any loose rust off 1st then attack with this stuff. Still haven't decided about paint vs rubberized undercoating? It would be easier I suppose to use the undercoating. It's been pretty hot here lately too but I'm thinking ahead because my next vaca is in January! It'll be too late to do anything about it then! Man...I wish I had a garage <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||14
